  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Living in West Carrollton means experiencing all four seasons, and each one leaves its own mark on your apartment. Winter brings salt and slush on every pair of boots. Spring brings pollen through every open window. Summer brings humidity that can make bathrooms musty, and fall brings leaves and the push to get ready for heating season. Instead of one exhausting deep clean a year, a seasonal routine keeps your place fresh with much less effort.</p>

<h2>Daily and weekly basics</h2>
<p>Seasonal cleaning works best on top of a simple routine. Each day, wipe kitchen counters, do the dishes, and put things back where they belong. Each week, vacuum or sweep floors, clean the bathroom sink and toilet, change bath towels, empty the trash and recycling, and wipe down high-touch spots like light switches and door handles. With that baseline in place, the seasonal lists below become manageable.</p>

<h2>Winter (December through February): fight salt, slush, and dry air</h2>
<p>Ohio winters mean road salt, gray slush, and a furnace running much of the day. Your focus is keeping the outside out and the inside healthy.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Set up an entry zone.</strong> A heavy doormat outside, an absorbent mat inside, and a boot tray catch most of the mess before it reaches your floors.</li>
<li><strong>Clean up salt residue promptly.</strong> Salt leaves white marks on hard floors and can damage carpet over time. Damp-mop hard floors with warm water and a little white vinegar, then dry. For carpet, vacuum and blot.</li>
<li><strong>Dust vents and baseboard heaters.</strong> Dust buildup reduces efficiency and can create a burning smell when heat kicks on.</li>
<li><strong>Wash heavy blankets and throws</strong> that get used more in winter.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for condensation</strong> on windows. Wipe it up to prevent mildew on sills, and let your landlord know if it is constant.</li>
<li><strong>Report heating problems right away.</strong> Do not wait for a small issue to become a cold night.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Spring (March through May): the big refresh</h2>
<p>Spring is the traditional time for a deeper clean, and in the Miami Valley it is also peak pollen season. Open the windows on mild days, then get to work.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wash windows inside</strong> and wipe window tracks, which collect grime and dead bugs over winter.</li>
<li><strong>Clean or wash curtains and blinds.</strong> Many curtains can go in the washer on a gentle cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Swap winter clothes out.</strong> Wash, dry, and store coats and sweaters in vacuum bags or bins. Donate anything you did not wear.</li>
<li><strong>Clean under and behind furniture.</strong> Move the couch, bed, and dressers if you can.</li>
<li><strong>Deep-clean the fridge.</strong> Toss expired condiments and wipe the shelves.</li>
<li><strong>Keep pollen down.</strong> Vacuum more often, wash bedding weekly, and consider a HEPA air purifier if you have allergies.</li>
<li><strong>Check the HVAC filter.</strong> Whether it is your job or the landlord's depends on your lease, but spring is a good time to make sure it gets done before cooling season.</li>
</ul>

<!--products-->

<h2>Summer (June through August): control moisture and bugs</h2>
<p>Southwest Ohio summers can be hot and sticky. Humidity is the main enemy indoors.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Run the bathroom fan</strong> during showers and for at least 15 to 20 minutes after. Squeegee or wipe down shower walls.</li>
<li><strong>Wash shower curtains and bath mats</strong> regularly to stop mildew.</li>
<li><strong>Use a dehumidifier</strong> in rooms that feel damp or smell musty, and keep the air conditioning running to help pull moisture from the air.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the kitchen crumb-free.</strong> Warm weather brings ants. Store food in sealed containers, rinse recyclables, and take out the trash often. Report pest problems to your landlord.</li>
<li><strong>Clean ceiling fan blades</strong> before turning them on high. An old pillowcase slipped over each blade catches the dust.</li>
<li><strong>Tidy your balcony or patio.</strong> Sweep, wipe down outdoor furniture, and check your lease for rules on grills and storage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fall (September through November): prep for heating season</h2>
<p>Fall is a good time to get your apartment ready for being indoors more.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dust everything up high:</strong> ceiling fans, light fixtures, the tops of cabinets and the refrigerator.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum vents and returns</strong> before you turn the heat on for the first time.</li>
<li><strong>Clean the oven</strong> ahead of holiday cooking. Check the manual if it is self-cleaning, and open windows for ventilation.</li>
<li><strong>Swap summer clothes for winter</strong> and wash your coats before you need them.</li>
<li><strong>Check smoke and carbon monoxide detectors.</strong> Press the test button and let your landlord know if one is not working.</li>
<li><strong>Clear leaves from your balcony or patio drain</strong> if you have one.</li>
<li><strong>Look for drafts</strong> around windows and doors. Our guide to <a href="/blog/lower-utility-bills-apartment-ohio/">lowering utility bills</a> covers easy fixes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cleaning hacks that save time</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clean top to bottom.</strong> Dust shelves and fans first, then wipe counters, then do the floors last.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a caddy</strong> with your most-used supplies so you can carry everything room to room.</li>
<li><strong>Microwave a bowl of water with lemon slices</strong> for a few minutes to loosen splatter before wiping.</li>
<li><strong>Use a timer.</strong> Fifteen focused minutes gets more done than an hour of drifting.</li>
<li><strong>Do one seasonal task per weekend</strong> instead of the whole list at once.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Cleaning and your security deposit</h2>
<p>Regular cleaning makes move-out far easier. Ohio Revised Code 5321.16 requires landlords to return the deposit, along with an itemized list of any deductions, within 30 days after the lease ends and you give up possession. Be sure to give your landlord a forwarding address in writing. Normal wear and tear should not be deducted, but excessive dirt or damage may be. This is not legal advice; see our <a href="/blog/ohio-tenant-rights-guide/">Ohio tenant rights guide</a> for more detail.</p>
<p>Pair your cleaning routine with our <a href="/blog/renter-maintenance-checklist/">renter maintenance checklist</a> to catch small problems early and keep your apartment in great shape all year.</p>
